WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.580
Your kidneys filter about ten grams of calcium a day.

2
00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:09.100
You lose about a fifth of a gram in urine.

3
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.700
So almost all the filtered calcium goes back into the blood.

4
00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.260
What sets that last small fraction?

5
00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:19.820
Work it out with made-up round numbers, for a reference adult at steady state.

6
00:00:20.783 --> 00:00:26.083
In adult plasma, roughly half the calcium is ionized, about 40 percent bound to

7
00:00:26.083 --> 00:00:28.863
protein, and about 10 percent complexed.

8
00:00:29.303 --> 00:00:31.503
Calcium bound to protein is not filtered.

9
00:00:32.103 --> 00:00:37.103
A GFR of 125 mL/min is 180 L a day.

10
00:00:37.923 --> 00:00:42.543
Total calcium is 100 mg/L, and 60 percent is filterable.

11
00:00:42.543 --> 00:00:46.363
So the filtered load is 10,800 mg per day.

12
00:00:47.383 --> 00:00:49.923
Most of that is reabsorbed along the tubule.

13
00:00:50.623 --> 00:00:55.523
Roughly 60 to 70 percent of filtered calcium is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule.

14
00:00:56.103 --> 00:01:00.883
20 to 25 percent is reabsorbed in the thick ascending limb, largely between cells.

15
00:01:01.663 --> 00:01:06.023
The distal convoluted and connecting tubules take back roughly 5 to 10 percent,

16
00:01:06.023 --> 00:01:12.483
through cells. Parathyroid hormone, or PTH, fine-tunes this small step and also acts

17
00:01:12.483 --> 00:01:13.483
upstream.

18
00:01:14.017 --> 00:01:17.697
Urinary calcium in the example is 200 mg per day.

19
00:01:18.257 --> 00:01:23.777
That is 1.85 percent of the filtered load, so 98.15 percent is reabsorbed.

20
00:01:24.317 --> 00:01:29.697
Now let reabsorption fall to 97 percent, with the same filtered load, as when PTH

21
00:01:29.697 --> 00:01:36.057
falls. Excretion becomes 324 mg per day, 1.62 times the original.

22
00:01:36.657 --> 00:01:42.117
A fall of about 1.15 percentage points moves excretion by 62

23
00:01:43.017 --> 00:01:47.917
The parathyroid chief cells carry the calcium-sensing receptor, which reads ionized

24
00:01:47.917 --> 00:01:53.457
calcium. A rise in ionized calcium activates the receptor and inhibits PTH

25
00:01:53.457 --> 00:01:58.817
secretion. The response is steep: a small change in calcium moves secretion a long

26
00:01:58.817 --> 00:02:03.497
way. PTH raises active calcium reabsorption in the distal nephron.

27
00:02:04.117 --> 00:02:09.337
So when PTH falls, distal reabsorption falls, and with the same filtered load,

28
00:02:09.517 --> 00:02:10.917
urinary calcium rises.

29
00:02:12.067 --> 00:02:19.027
Keep the rule. The kidney filters about 10 g of calcium a day and reabsorbs about 98

30
00:02:19.027 --> 00:02:23.547
The distal step that PTH regulates handles under a tenth of the load.

31
00:02:24.227 --> 00:02:27.987
Yet that step fine-tunes urine calcium, along with filtered load.
